it turns out that yahoo was letting the  NSA read your email even before you  opened it Yahoo Inc last year secretly  built a custom software program to  search all of its customers incoming  email for specific information provided  by US intelligence officials the company  complied with a classified US government  demand and what they would do is scan  hundreds of millions of yahoo mail  accounts at the request of the National  Security Agency or FBI and this is all  according to three former employees and  a fourth person who knew about this  there are some surveillance experts who  are saying this is the first time that  we're seeing a u.s. internet company  agreeing to an intelligence agencies  request by searching all arriving  messages as opposed to examining stored  messages are scanning a small number of  accounts in real time let me translate  in layman's terms Yahoo was scanning the  messages and reporting to the NSA about  those messages before the recipients  even read them now it's not known  specifically what information  intelligence officials were looking for  only that they wanted yahoo to search  for a set of characters this could be a  phrase in an email some kind of a phrase  or wording in an attachment uh unclear  exactly what it means but this is sort  of the gist of it and Reuters was uh not  able to figure out what Yahoo may have  handed over so if we sort of imagine a  tiered system you've got all yahoo email  being scanned by these uh filters then  you have some specific words phrases or  signatures that were being looked for  and then you have some portion of that  and it could be a hundred percent of  that being turned over to authorities  now this it's it's known that US phone  and Internet companies are known to hand  over bulk customer data to intelligence  agencies but there are some former  government officials and private  surveillance experts who said they have  never seen or heard  of such broad real-time scanning  collection and reporting of customer  emails and data and as soon as this  happened as I sort of mentioned earlier  my interview with bruce schneier  Microsoft Twitter Google Facebook Apple  they all very quickly put out statements  saying oh we don't do anything like this  we haven't received requests like this  we haven't complied with requests like  this as Bruce Schneier mentioned in our  interview earlier in today's show he is  skeptical of that to say the least and  Apple specifically pointed to a recent  statement made by their te CEO Tim Cook  which said I want to be absolutely clear  that we have never worked with any  government agency from any country to  create a backdoor in any of our products  or services we have also never allowed  access to our servers and we never will  here's the way in which the wording is  so important this is what Bruce Schneier  was referring to if we look at this  statement from Apple now this is all  speculative I want to be clear if you  look at was it what he says he says we  have never worked with a government  agency to create a backdoor okay that  might mean they created the back door on  their own without working with a  government agency or that they worked  with the government agency to provide  data in a way that isn't through a back  door right this is why Bruce Schneier is  saying these denials need to be looked  at really closely and his last sentence  we have also never allowed access to our  servers and we never will well you can  very very easily mirror what is on your  servers or parts of it on to some other  shared server that Apple and the NSA  might have shared access to and you  would be right you have never allowed  the NSA access to your servers but it  doesn't really represent the spirit of  what it is Tim Cook is saying I'm not  saying this is what Apple was doing I I  merely want to have the conversation  with you about how these are worded in a  way where anything could really be going  on meanwhile Yahoo put out a statement  that doesn't deny that the program  exists they're not really denying any of  this it's a pretty but milquetoast and  generic statement and this is really  really concerning and it might be  interesting for you to know that  at one point Yahoo was fined for not  participating in prism which was another  one of these sort of surveillance  security type initiatives they were  fined for not participating at one point  in the past and now we learn they are  participating in a different way uh in a  way that exceeds even our wildest  expectations of what was going on so  very very concerning stuff from Yahoo
 

